3:> Status: foundation frozen, October 20, 2026. D149 adds extraction-owned grouping; D165 puts it in the default pipeline. D169: the matching step runs on Jev by default (`poc-v23`); D168 states the Replace row's full definition and the succession answer it needs. English edition: October 25, 2026 (D163). Only recorded decisions apply; open questions and two pull-mode proposals remain separate.
8:A thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date. Evidence moves its state, which freezes when due. Search then compare candidates with existing theses to merge, continue, create or skip; compare evidence within its thesis to repeat, replace or add. Extra source references alone do not trigger rejudgement. Extraction determines group associations; the writer preserves them after resolving final thesis IDs under the [grouping contract](#research-groups-associate-during-extraction).
12:- Clear structures connect theses, ladders and evidence to source items.
14:- A fixed, verifiable ladder states in advance what would disprove the view, analogous to explicit analyst scenarios or predefined investment exit conditions. Do not change it after observing the outcome.
16:- A theme's outcome can help one asset and harm another. Theme ladders describe outcomes; asset dependencies specify their own direction.
17:- A thesis contains one period and one due date, generally 6–12 months; intermediate quarterly reports are evidence. A thesis on a binding obligation a universe company takes on (a guarantee, financing, an equity stake, a purchase commitment) may set its own check day up to 18 months after the item, because the signing can be checked long before what it finances is built (D185).
18:- Search finds plausible matches and the model compares their meaning. A match inherits the existing ladder and due date; a merely similar question is new. D155's current POC additionally gives code ownership of fully determined duplicate identity, with its limits in the improvement plan.
32:│   └─ Thesis → A question + a ladder + a due date (Immutable once written. Five rungs, or two for yes/no. When matching opens a thesis, only one may be open for the same subject and focus at a time.)
33:│       ├─ State → The thesis's current ladder rung (Evidence pushes towards either end; the model judges from facts. A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it.)
35:│       ├─ Dependency → Another thesis (Direction specifies which end of the other thesis is bullish for an asset thesis, or whether the two ladders have the same or opposite orientation for a theme thesis.)
36:│       └─ Succession → The previous period's thesis (Successive periods of the same focus form a chain)
50:The same event can affect assets differently: rate cuts can be bullish for Ethereum and bearish for JPM net interest income. Theme theses therefore do not label outcomes good or bad.
55:<!-- generated: ladder -->
57:Two kinds of ladder (Illustrative ranges)
72:1. A ladder has five rungs. Asset theses run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish, describing business impact; theme theses name five ordered outcomes without good/bad labels. Use two rungs for yes/no rather than forcing five: bullish/bearish for assets; named outcomes for themes, such as signed by the deadline/not signed.
74:3. One ladder concerns one thing. Separate numeric and event questions and connect them with dependencies. Likewise, separate components that can move in opposite directions, such as H20 and Blackwell chip classes.
75:4. The extraction model writes the candidate's ladder. A new thesis adopts it unchanged until expiry. A merged candidate uses the existing thesis's ladder and due date.
76:5. Numeric theses name a year. Non-company quantities use calendar years: the publication year is this year and the following year is next year, each ending December 31. Company quantities use the fiscal year containing publication day and its successor. Never use the computer clock. For a given quantity, open at most one thesis for each of this year and next year; do not open later years. The due date is the quantity's publication date.
77:6. Set the due date when the debate can be resolved, usually 6–12 months away. Intermediate quarterly reports are evidence, not separate theses.
78:<!-- /generated: ladder -->
106:- Store evidence once without an intrinsic direction. Each rejudgement writes its push on the evidence–thesis link. One statement can affect multiple theses differently.
113:B depends on A | What kind of thesis is A? (For an asset thesis B, state which end of A is bullish for B. For a theme thesis B, state whether their ladders have the same or opposite orientation.)
125:- A dependency has one direction. An apparent “good first, bad later” relationship needs an intermediate thesis: steel tariffs raising domestic steel prices and weakening downstream steel demand are separate paths.
126:- Dependencies and the theme block are the two ways theses influence each other; succession only orders periods. B depending on A means B reads A's conclusion. The theme block (D191, D195) shows every open, judged theme thesis to every asset thesis's judgement, one to all, and the judgement states which way each theme it weighed pushes it; no edge is written for it. Keep B's direct evidence separate from dependency states and reasons. A's conclusion can affect B like evidence but is not stored as evidence: external events and statements are evidence, whereas A is our own judgement. Treating it as evidence would let the system convince itself.
133:## Succession: the same focus over successive periods
136:- Successive periods of the same focus form a chain, such as Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 rate → 2028 rate. The chain records period order. Dependency traversal follows the rules above.
138:- Keep ladder orientation across succession: a strongly bullish first rung remains strongly bullish, although thresholds can change. Dependencies retain their direction; reversing the ends would break it.
148:| Chain | Succession: Fed year-end 2026 rate → 2027 → 2028 | Replacement: AMZN capex guidance 12% growth → 25% |
163:  ↓ 4 Match (Model): The model does not read the original item. Show each existing thesis's subject, question, focus, ladder, due date and open/closed state. In one call: (1) decide candidate identity or a narrower part, succession with unchanged ladder orientation but new thresholds, new thesis for distinct or merely similar questions, or skip overly broad questions, numbers beyond this/next year and the same period of a closed thesis; give reasons; (2) choose dependencies and directions among searched, block-5 and same-item new theses; (3) compare evidence linked only to existing theses. Evidence linked to candidates waits for their destinations, then searches all linked existing theses together and uses one further comparison call. Evidence linked only to new theses is new evidence.
175:| Merge into existing | The candidate concerns the same focus or a part of it, such as H20 China revenue within China revenue. Merge only into open theses; closed theses can only be succeeded. | Compare its evidence within the target thesis and preserve grouping under this section's rules. Use the existing ladder and due date. |
176:| Succession | The next period of the same focus: the next year for numbers, or continued tracking after an event thesis closed | Create a thesis and record succession |
177:| New thesis | Distinct or merely similar: Blackwell export licences and H20 export licences are different questions | Create a thesis with its ladder and due date fixed from now on |
178:| Skip | Too broad or mixed, bundling questions that can move oppositely, such as all US chip controls on China; numbers beyond this/next year; the same focus and period as a closed thesis; or an out-of-universe subject. Proposed themes go to human review; out-of-universe companies receive only a processing-log entry. | Do not create it. Evidence also linked to a specific valid thesis still counts; evidence linked only to the skipped candidate is not stored. |
180:- The D155 POC uses code for fully determined duplicate identity and model comparison when scope or legacy identity needs judgement. A shared quantity (an aggregate opened under its admission reason) is one thesis per theme, measure and period: its population or wording is not identity, so code merges another wording into the open one and succeeds it for the next period (D197). Reference lists guide research without vetoing candidates. Same group does not mean same thesis. See docs/poc/grouping-improvement-plan.md for the contract and measured scope.
181:- Search finds plausible candidates; the model handles semantic comparison. H20 and Blackwell export licences can look similar but are separate questions. Bullish and bearish accounts of one question share its identity.
192:| New evidence | Anything else: a new event, another period's figure or another speaker's statement, including disagreement. Uncertainty also means new evidence. | Add it alongside existing evidence and rejudge |
