242:  ↓ 3 Human labelling (Person): Label two sets: code-refused decisions from validation step 5 and periodic random samples. People supply the correct outcome.
289:| Succession | Predecessor, if any | Succeeds FY2026 |
300:| Standalone summary | A sentence understandable without the original: who, what, how much and which period. Matching and rejudgement read it. |
305:| Event time | When the event happened or takes effect; for a number, the period it describes |
316:- Append-only records: questions, ladders and due dates are immutable. Matching, rejudgement, succession, closure, reopening and labels append records with sequence numbers.
317:- The timeline only appends, never rewrites past knowledge. A late statement already superseded by a newer one is logged as superseded and unused, not added to the thesis. Previously unknown information counts normally with its true date. The evidence-matching model distinguishes these cases; add no separate rule.
332:| Final verdict | Whether it remains separate is deferred; due-date freezing is decided. |
339:- Every thesis has an immutable question, ladder and due date; five rungs or two for yes/no, with exhaustive, non-overlapping, verifiable outcomes.
357:- Design pull mode later: day-end/scheduled execution, overall review, and whether final verdict remains a separate step.
365:1. Proposal for later pull-mode design: besides due-date freezing, distinguish an unavailable answer (quantity no longer disclosed or event ceases to exist; dependants lack that input) and pending publication (period ended but the number is not yet released).
366:2. Proposal for later pull-mode design: daily pairwise duplicate checks among open theses of a subject; merge the later thesis into the earlier, move links and dependencies, check cycles and rejudge. Add separate duplicate-check settings to Config.
374:- D38–D40: question/ladder/due-date structure, state, evidence links/tiers, dependencies/succession, round-based intake, history and matching labels.
