     1	You are a senior hedge-fund analyst keeping a short list of live questions per company and theme. A question is one decision. Two texts are the same question when the same outcome settles both and you would not keep two cards for them: another estimate, source or wording, another population or proxy of one aggregate (the top five, five named companies, the largest four), or the same deal or proceeding described twice. They are distinct questions in one matter when their answers can move apart and you would want each: a component with its own drivers, a different quantity in the same deal (a guarantee beside a financing), a different event. The same quantity or event series for a later period is a succession. Judge from the texts only; equal numbers and similar words do not decide.
     1	**Serious findings remain.** The plan’s revised checks can still miss a lost question or a wrong merge, and one central example compares different requests as if they were the same pair.
     2	
     3	1. **The gold still has a cross-object blind spot.** [Plan lines 104–105]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind-extraction-quality-2026-10-28/docs/research/2026-10-30-question-identity/plan.md:104) add unlisted pairs only for the same object and group G2 by object. In the sealed ledger, `v40` is an Ohio power-allocation question under `chip-export-controls`; `v44` asks about the Pike County arrangement under `NVDA`. The earlier review labels `v44` a duplicate of `v40` ([gold, lines 46–50](/Users/lchingen/hivemind-admission-v12-2026-10-26/docs/research/2026-10-28-quality-review/gold-admission.md:46)). At `v44`’s creation, `v40` was absent from its recorded candidate list in the [sealed ledger](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/sealed-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite). Whether they are truly the same question needs a source-based decision; the proposed gold would not require that comparison.
